<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xml:id="sec-acidsbases2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Naming acids and bases</title>

	<p>
		We already know how to name the common bases that we'll see in this chapter.
		Most of the bases we'll be discussing are ionic compounds that contain the
		hydroxide ion.
		We'll be naming those compounds in the same way that we've named them in the
		past.
		There is one additional base that we'll frequently encounter in this chapter,
		<m>NH_3</m>, which is called ammonia (you should learn this).
		We'll learn about other nitrogen containing bases in later chapters as well as
		how to name them.
	</p>

	<p>
		The rules for naming acids depend on the type of acid:
	</p>

	<ol>
		<li>
			<p>
				Acids composed of two elements (binary acids).
			</p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p>
				Acids containing a polyatomic ion.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<p>
				Organic acids (discussed in a later chapter).
			</p>
		</li>
	</ol>

	<p>
		Acids that contain only two elements include <m>HCl</m>, <m>HBr</m>, and
		<m>HI</m>.
		For these acids, we start with the name of the anion (for example, chloride
		for <m>HCl</m>).
		We then drop the -ide ending and replace it with “-ic acid”.
		Lastly, we add “hydro-” as a prefix.
		Thus, <m>HCl</m> is hydrochloric acid.
	</p>

	<p>
		If the acid contains a polyatomic ion, then we name the anion and drop the
		-ate ending from the name (we won't worry about polyatomic ions with other
		endings).
		We then add “-ic acid” and in some cases instead add “-uric acid” or “-oric
		acid” (if the name of the polyatomic ion ends in an “f” sound; ex.
		-fate or -phate).
	</p>

	<p>
		Additionally, some acids are polyprotic acids, meaning that they have more
		than one <m>H^+</m> that can be donated to a base.
		Phosphoric acid, for example, is the acid produced from the phosphate ion,
		<m>PO_4^{3-}</m>.
		The acid therefore requires three <m>H^+</m> ions to neutralize its charge,
		giving the formula <m>H_3PO_4</m>.
		Previously, we learned that there are other forms of the phosphate anion that
		have one or two <m>H^+</m> ions added.
		<m>HPO_4^{2-}</m> and <m>H_2PO_4^-</m> are called hydrogen phosphate and
		dihydrogen phosphate, respectively.
		These ions can also act as acids because they can donate an <m>H^+</m> ion.
		Their names remain those of the corresponding polyatomic ions.
	</p>

	<p>
		To figure out the formula for an acid, we need to know the anion that it is
		paired with, both its formula and its charge.
		We then only need to pair it with the appropriate number of <m>H^+</m> ions in
		order to have the number of positive and negative charges be equal.
	</p>

	<exercises>
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					What is the name of the following acids?
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HBr</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Hydrobromic acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HI</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Hydroiodic acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HNO_3</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Nitric acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>H_3PO_4</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Phosphoric acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>H_2SO_4</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Sulfuric acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HCO_3^-</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Hydrogen carbonate or bicarbonate
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					What is the name of the following bases?
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>KOH</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Potassium hydroxide
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>Sr(OH)_2</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Strontium hydroxide
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>NH_3</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Ammonia
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HCO_3^-</m>
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Hydrogen carbonate or bicarbonate
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						<m>HCO_3^-</m> is listed as an acid in problem 1f and a base in problem
						2d.
						How can it be both?
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						It can accept an <m>H^+</m> to become carbonic acid; it can also donate an
						<m>H^+</m> to become carbonate.
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					What is the correct chemical formula for the following acids or bases?
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Calcium hydroxide
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>Ca(OH)_2</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Carbonic acid
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>H_2CO_3</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Acetic acid
					</p>
				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>
						The acetate ion is a polyatomic ion that you should know...
					</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>CH_3CO_2H</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Hydrochloric acid
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>HCl</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Magnesium hydroxide
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>Mg(OH)_2</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Cesium hydroxide
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<m>CsOH</m>
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					What is the correct acid name when <m>H^+</m> ions are added to the
					following polyatomic ions to form acids?
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Citrate
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Citric acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Aspartate
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Aspartic acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Oxalate
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						Oxalic acid
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
	</exercises>
</section>